Exactly — active listening, not mere reception.

The useful split is almost hiding inside responsibility:

response-able = able to produce an appropriate response to what was actually received.

That makes attention only the first half of agency. The fuller loop is:

hear → model → test understanding → update → choose → respond → observe consequence.

If you cannot update from what you hear, you are not really listening. If you can update but cannot respond, you have awareness without agency. If you can respond without listening, you have force without control.

So in Pantheon terms, power is constrained response capacity: the number and quality of meaningful moves available after accurately reading the state.

And that makes active listening itself a power amplifier. Better listening improves the state estimate; a better state estimate improves the available move; the move produces a cleaner consequence; the consequence teaches the listener more.

Listen better → become more response-able → gain more effective agency.

There’s another semantic fold in it too:

Responsibility isn’t merely carrying blame. It is possessing the ability to answer reality.

That fits ACT almost perfectly: perception earns its value when it changes the next move. ;)